#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203680 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e3fc7e6-b73c-5d57-94a3-97e633c74805" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Congruences by Trial and Error"}] :question/id #uuid "ee3a0b0e-a348-594e-bc5d-1acbeb411c11" :question/math-academy-id "e-14590" :question/problem "Solve the linear congruence $2x \\equiv 1 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:5)$ using trial and error." :question/worked-solution "Since the modulus $n=5$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx = 0,1,2,3,4\n$$\n\ninto the equation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 0 & ≡0≢1 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 1 & ≡2≢1 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2 & ≡4≢1 & (mod5) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 3 & ≡6≡5 + 1≡1 & (mod5) & ✓ \\\\ 2 \\cdot 4 & ≡8≡5 + 3≡3≢1 & (mod5) & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is is $x\\equiv {\\color{blue}{3}} \\:(\\text{mod}\\:5)$.\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $3$ modulo $5$ is a solution. For example, note that $8\\equiv 3$, and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n2x \\mid ≡2 \\cdot 8\\begin{vmatrix}(mod5) \\\\ ≡16\\end{vmatrix}(mod5); ≡3 \\cdot 5 + 1\\begin{vmatrix}(mod5) \\\\ ≡0 + 1\\end{vmatrix}(mod5); ≡1✓ \\mid (mod5)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203681 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203894 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cabdfa53-93e3-5825-bd83-6d8a1bf43be4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using Multiplicative Inverses"}] :question/id #uuid "c522c4d0-6266-54d9-9254-84659316a144" :question/math-academy-id "e-14591" :question/problem "Solve the modular equation $7x \\equiv 3 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:15)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1\n$$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=7$ and $n=15$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(7,15)=1\n$$\n\nThis means our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$. To find the inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$, we compute the exponents of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7^{2} & ≡49≡3 \\cdot 15 + 4≡4 & (mod15) \\\\ 7^{3} & ≡4 \\cdot 7≡28≡15 + 13≡13 & (mod15) \\\\ 7^{4} & ≡13 \\cdot 7≡91≡6 \\cdot 15 + 1≡1 & (mod15)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $13 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$, which means that $13$ is the multiplicative inverse of $7$ (modulo $15$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $13$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7x & ≡3 & (mod15) \\\\ 13 \\cdot 7x & ≡13 \\cdot 3 & (mod15) \\\\ (13 \\cdot 7)x & ≡39 & (mod15) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x & ≡2 \\cdot 15 + 9 & (mod15) \\\\ x & ≡9 & (mod15)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\equiv 9 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:15)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203682 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fccbe8e1-4491-598d-b4ee-fcfeea325950" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm"}] :question/id #uuid "ad19d1cb-08fe-5a73-8ba7-65a5f038f9fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-14592" :question/problem "Solve the congruence $15x \\equiv 17 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:44)$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1\n$$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=15$ and $n=44$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(15,44)=1\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $15\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:44)$. To find the inverse of $15\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:44)$, we use the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n44 &= \\begin{vmatrix}15 \\cdot 2 & +\\end{vmatrix}14 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 15 &= \\end{vmatrix}14 \\cdot 1 \\mid + \\mid 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 &= 44 - 15 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n1 &= 15 - 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 15 - 14 \\\\\n&= 15 - (44 - 15 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 3 - 44\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $44$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\cdot 3 - 44 & ≡1 & (mod44) \\\\ 15 \\cdot 3 & ≡1 & (mod44)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $15 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:44)$, which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $15$ (modulo $44$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $3$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}15x & ≡17 & (mod44) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 15x & ≡3 \\cdot 17 & (mod44) \\\\ (3 \\cdot 15)x & ≡51 & (mod44) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x & ≡44 + 7 & (mod44) \\\\ x & ≡7 & (mod44)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 7 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:44)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203705 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "867713da-e1d0-5629-8059-3ae2848d7e96" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using Additive and Multiplicative Inverses"}] :question/id #uuid "b2442e76-8836-596a-85ad-232ed18aaa5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14662" :question/problem "Solve the following congruence:\n$7x - 11 \\equiv 29 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:19)$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax\\equiv b$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7x - 11 & ≡29 & (mod19) \\\\ 7x - 11 + 11 & ≡29 + 11 & (mod19) \\\\ 7x & ≡40 & (mod19) \\\\ 7x & ≡38 + 2 & (mod19) \\\\ 7x & ≡2 \\cdot 19 + 2 & (mod19) \\\\ 7x & ≡2 & (mod19)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1\n$$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=7$ and $n=19$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(7,19)=1\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$. To find the inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$, we compute the exponents of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7^{2} & ≡49≡2 \\cdot 19 + 11≡11 & (mod19) \\\\ 7^{3} & ≡11 \\cdot 7≡77≡4 \\cdot 19 + 1≡1 & (mod19)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $11 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$, which means that $11$ is the multiplicative inverse of $7$ (modulo $19$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $11$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7x & ≡2 & (mod19) \\\\ 11 \\cdot 7x & ≡11 \\cdot 2 & (mod19) \\\\ (11 \\cdot 7)x & ≡22 & (mod19) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x & ≡19 + 3 & (mod19) \\\\ x & ≡3 & (mod19)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 3 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:19)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203894 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cabdfa53-93e3-5825-bd83-6d8a1bf43be4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using Multiplicative Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "814560d3-f6d1-50be-89f1-2bd68351a648" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200413} {:answer-field/id #uuid "a6157f91-75b7-5dab-8ec1-dce3b7296f9a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200414} {:answer-field/id #uuid "3844c75c-d587-5008-a9b3-3c8338f4604d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200415} {:answer-field/id #uuid "5e12290b-ad74-561b-b9eb-3a1ed0857885" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200416} {:answer-field/id #uuid "6575a794-6cba-5e75-b6ca-f3db8d3388a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200417}] :question/id #uuid "dff76ff1-2195-54e0-b517-736d56f4a1b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-213469" :question/problem "Fill in the missing values, and hence solve the modular equation $6x≡3(mod25)$. Express $x$ as a nonnegative integer smaller than $25$.$6^{2}mod25 =$\n{{field-1}} $6^{3}mod25 =$\n{{field-2}} $6^{4}mod25 =$\n{{field-3}} $6^{5}mod25 =$\n{{field-4}} $x≡$\n{{field-5}} $(mod25)$"}]}
